.TH READ 2
.SH NAME
read \- read from file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B "read(fildes, buffer, nbytes)"
.B "char *buffer;"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IR fildes ,
a file descriptor, is an integer returned by a successful
.BR open ,
.BR creat ,
.BR dup ,
or
.BR pipe (2)
call.
.I buffer
is the location of
.I nbytes
contiguous bytes
into which the input will be placed.
It is not guaranteed that all
.I nbytes
bytes will be read; for example if the file refers to a terminal at most one
line will be returned.
In any event the number of characters read is
returned.
.PP
If the returned value is 0, then end-of-file has been reached immediately,
with no bytes read.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
open(2), creat(2), dup(2), pipe(2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
As mentioned, 0 is returned when the end of the file has been reached.
If the read was otherwise unsuccessful the return value is \-1.
Many
conditions can generate an error: physical I/O errors, bad buffer address,
preposterous
.IR nbytes ,
file descriptor not that of an input file.
.SH ASSEMBLER
(read = 3.)
.TP
PDP-11:
.nf
R0 = fildes
sys read; buffer; nbytes
R0 = byte count
.fi
.TP
8086:
.nf
BX = 3
AX = fildes
DX = buffer
CX = nbytes
int 0Xf1
AX = byte count
.fi
.sp
